The trace below lists the steps that an agent ran on a small file store. The store holds the teaching material and grade lists of a university course. All steps ran. The source file of every delete, rename, copy and move was in the store when the step started, a set writes its file whether or not it exists yet, and each step changed the store.

Files at the start, with their content:
  archive/2025/attendance.csv = archived
  archive/2025/lecture_01.md = archived
  archive/assignment_2.md = archived
  assignments/assignment_1_old.md = outdated
  assignments/grades.csv = raw
  assignments/lecture_01.md = approved
  backup/lecture_01_draft.md = v1
  drafts/lecture_01_draft.md = draft
  handouts/lecture_03.md = approved
  handouts/syllabus.md = approved
  lectures/lecture_01_old.md = outdated
  tmp/scratch.tmp = scratch

Meaning of the steps (arguments in the order given in the trace):
  set: stores the given content under the path, creating the file or replacing the content of the existing one.
  delete: the file at the path is gone afterwards.
  rename: the file keeps its folder and content and takes the new name; an existing file of that name is lost.
  copy: creates or overwrites the destination with the content of the source, which stays in place.
  move: transfers the file into the target folder with the same name, replacing a file of that name if present; nothing is left at the old path.

The agent was asked to reach these conditions, and every one of them must hold after the last step (file counts refer to the files directly inside a folder, not those in sub-folders):
  G1. folder archive holds 4 files
  G2. handouts/lecture_01.md contains final
