Below are the integrity rules for sensor reading records and one batch of 16 records to be checked against them. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: reading_id (text), alarm_code (text), valid_samples (whole number), humidity (whole number), probe (text), script_id (text), recheck_of (text), cycle (text), gateway (text), operator (text), all_samples (whole number), frame_no (whole number), taken_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), sensor (text), void_samples (whole number), uploaded_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), alarm (text).

Rules:
R1. No record has a `uploaded_on` that lies before its `taken_on`; the two may coincide.
R2. `alarm_code` may be empty only if `alarm` is not "yes".
R3. A `gateway` is valid only if the reference list `gateways` holds it and marks it "active".
R4. No two records agree in both `cycle` and `probe`; agreeing in just one of the two is fine.
R5. `recheck_of` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `reading_id` found in one of the records above it.
R6. `sensor` must be one of the keys of the reference list `sensors`.
R7. `all_samples` must equal `valid_samples` plus `void_samples`.
R8. The lowest allowed `humidity` is 5, the highest is 80.
R9. The values of `frame_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.

Reference list `gateways` with 9 entries (key: status): GW-15: active; GW-18: inactive; GW-19: active; GW-29: inactive; GW-56: suspended; GW-70: active; GW-74: active; GW-75: inactive; GW-92: active

Reference list `sensors` with 10 keys: SN-153, SN-185, SN-283, SN-321, SN-374, SN-391, SN-527, SN-712, SN-985, SN-991

Statuses in a reference list: a rule that only asks for a key of the list accepts every key the list holds, whatever its status; only a rule that names a status requires it.

