A loader validates parcel records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (10 records) are shown below. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Fields per record: parcel_id (text), door_code (text), signature (text), receiver (text), scan_no (whole number), delivered_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), sent_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), gross_weight (whole number), forwarded_from (text), manifest (text), locker_id (text), bag (text), pieces (whole number), piece_weight (whole number), route (text).

Rules to check:
R1. Each `parcel_id` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `parcel_id` breaks this rule.
R2. `route` must be a key of the reference list `routes` whose status there is "approved".
R3. Whenever `signature` is "yes", `receiver` must be filled in.
R4. `delivered_on` falls on the day of `sent_on` or on a later day.
R5. `door_code` and `locker_id` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R6. `scan_no` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R7. `gross_weight` is the product of `pieces` and `piece_weight`; any other stored value breaks the rule.

Reference list `routes` with 7 entries, one per line (key: status):
RT-14: revoked
RT-37: approved
RT-39: approved
RT-41: approved
RT-48: approved
RT-70: approved
RT-74: revoked

A listed key is a listed key, whatever its status: only a rule that names a status asks for it, and any other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys.

